 ### Boundary-Constrained Synthesis
-Boundary-Constrained Synthesis is a method to synthesize scenes of arbitrary size without artifacts, specifically preventing artifacts at the boundaries resulting from obscured parts of the radiance field [S2]. It avoids artifacts resulting from obscured parts of the radiance field [S2]. The method divides the radiance field into five parts: upper boundary $B_u$, lower $B_d$, left $B_l$, right $B_r$, and interior $I$. Boundary length is $l_b$. Four corner patches from the original field are placed onto corresponding corners of the synthesized result. Each of the four boundaries is synthesized using corresponding boundary patches from input samples (viewed as synthesizing individual strips). The synthesized boundaries are used as constraints for synthesizing the interior region using example patches from interior area $I$ [S2]. A pitfall of the two-phase method is that it fails at boundaries because occluded interior parts are synthesized onto the boundary, exhibiting artifacts due to lack of pixel value supervision during DVGO training [S2].
